{n:1,q:'Liver disease can impair regulation of which trace minerals handled importantly through bile?',o:['Iron and zinc','Selenium and molybdenum','Copper and manganese','Iodine and chromium'],a:2,e:'Copper and manganese homeostasis relies substantially on liver processing and biliary excretion.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:2,q:'Even without genetic disease, supplements or environmental overexposure, deficiency or excess can still occur for:',o:['Iron and iodine','Selenium and molybdenum','Copper and manganese','Zinc and chromium'],a:0,e:'Iron balance varies with losses and life stage, while iodine intake can be too low or high depending on diet and fortification.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:3,q:'Which statement about trace minerals is most accurate?',o:['They occur in only a few foods','All are regulated only by absorption','For most people, deficiencies and toxicities are uncommon','No trace mineral can cause toxicity'],a:2,e:'Most balanced diets provide trace minerals safely, but deficiency and toxicity are possible in specific contexts.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:4,q:'Which is a general characteristic of trace minerals?',o:['Plant content never varies with soil','Cooking never affects mineral content','They circulate from the GI tract to liver and other tissues','Food-source toxicities are common'],a:2,e:'Absorbed trace minerals enter circulation and are distributed through the liver and other tissues; specific routes vary.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:5,q:'Which organ is an important regulation and excretion site for iodine and selenium?',o:['Stomach','Small intestine','Kidneys','Liver'],a:2,e:'Urinary excretion through the kidneys is important for both iodine and selenium balance.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:6,q:'Which pair is substantially excreted in urine?',o:['Iron and iodine','Copper and manganese','Iodine and selenium','Selenium and manganese'],a:2,e:'Iodine and selenium are regulated in part through urinary excretion.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:7,q:'Enzymes that require a metal ion are called:',o:['Zymogens','Trace enzymes','Metalloenzymes','Substrates'],a:2,e:'A metalloenzyme contains a metal required for catalytic function.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:8,q:'What can improve iron absorption from a three-bean salad?',o:['Milk','Orange juice','Tea','Yogurt'],a:1,e:'Vitamin C in orange juice enhances nonheme iron absorption.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:9,q:'Which snack is richest in nonheme iron among these choices?',o:['Bread-crumb stuffed mushrooms','Non-fat yogurt with fruit','Potato chips','Pretzels with peanut butter'],a:3,e:'Peanut butter and enriched pretzels together provide more nonheme iron than the other listed snacks.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:10,q:'What factor in animal tissue can enhance nonheme iron bioavailability?',o:['Meat factor','Beef factor','Protein factor','Intrinsic factor'],a:0,e:'The “meat factor” describes the enhancing effect of meat, poultry and fish on nonheme iron absorption.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:11,q:'Which listed food contains polyphenols?',o:['Tea','Chocolate','Blueberries','Tofu'],a:0,e:'Tea is the source-intended answer, but all four can contain polyphenolic compounds. Because the item has multiple defensible answers, it is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:12,q:'By what fixed percentage does one cup of coffee or tea always reduce nonheme iron absorption?',o:['20–50%','30–60%','40–70%','50–80%'],a:3,e:'D reflects the source range, but there is no universal percentage. Beverage type, dose, meal composition, timing and iron status change the effect. This item is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:13,q:'The two iron storage forms are ferritin and:',o:['Hemoglobin','Transferrin','Albumin','Hemosiderin'],a:3,e:'Ferritin is the main storage protein; hemosiderin is a less readily mobilized storage aggregate.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:14,q:'Which iron-containing protein is found in muscle cells?',o:['Hemoglobin','Myoglobin','Albumin','Hemosiderin'],a:1,e:'Myoglobin binds oxygen in muscle.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:15,q:'Who is least likely to be iron deficient?',o:['A one-year-old','A six-year-old','A pregnant 30-year-old','A 41-year-old man'],a:3,e:'Among these examples, an adult man without described blood loss has the lowest typical iron-deficiency risk.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:16,q:'Severe iron deficiency causes which anemia pattern?',o:['Microcytic, hypochromic','Macrocytic, normochromic','Pernicious','Megaloblastic'],a:0,e:'Iron-deficient red cells are typically smaller and contain less hemoglobin.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:17,q:'Which food is the most concentrated copper source listed?',o:['Liver','Poultry','Broccoli','Winter squash'],a:0,e:'Organ meats, especially liver, are rich in copper.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:18,q:'Copper homeostasis occurs partly through adjustments in:',o:['Sweat loss','Absorption','Urine loss','Ionization'],a:1,e:'Intestinal absorption adjusts with copper status; biliary excretion is also central.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:19,q:'Iodine uptake by the thyroid is regulated chiefly by:',o:['Thyroid-regulating hormone','Thyroid-stimulating hormone','Goitrogen','Thyroxine'],a:1,e:'TSH stimulates thyroid iodide uptake and hormone synthesis.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:20,q:'Goitrogenic compounds that can inhibit thyroid iodine use occur in:',o:['Cruciferous vegetables','Citrus fruits','Red meats','Milk products'],a:0,e:'Crucifers contain glucosinolate-derived compounds, but ordinary intake is generally safe when iodine status is adequate.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:21,q:'Iodine deficiency is not expected to cause:',o:['Severe neurodevelopmental impairment','Excessive growth in stature','Infertility','Higher mortality risk in severe deficiency'],a:1,e:'Iodine deficiency impairs, rather than accelerates, normal growth and neurodevelopment.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:22,q:'Which is a selenoprotein?',o:['Thyroxine','Superoxide dismutase','Cytochrome c oxidase','Glutathione peroxidase'],a:3,e:'Several glutathione peroxidases contain selenium as selenocysteine.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:23,q:'Which mineral was historically designated essential after animal deficiency produced a diabetic-like state?',o:['Chromium','Zinc','Iodine','Magnesium'],a:0,e:'Chromium is the historical intended answer. Current NIH guidance says scientists do not presently consider it necessary for good health, so this item is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:24,q:'Excess absorbed chromium is excreted mainly:',o:['Through sweat','Via the lungs','In urine','In feces'],a:2,e:'Absorbed chromium is excreted mainly in urine.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:25,q:'Severe deficiency of which trace mineral can impair growth, skin and bone formation?',o:['Copper','Magnesium','Manganese','Iron'],a:2,e:'Severe manganese deficiency in experimental or unusual clinical settings can impair growth and skeletal development.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:26,q:'Which pairing supplies a cofactor for enzymes such as sulfite oxidase and aldehyde oxidase?',o:['Molybdenum; peas, grains and nuts','Zinc; milk, meat and seafood','Chromium; fruit, vegetables and grains','Selenium; meat, seafood and nuts'],a:0,e:'Molybdenum is a cofactor in several oxidases, and legumes, grains and nuts are useful sources.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:27,q:'Ferritin and metallothionein are proteins that can:',o:['Promote iron and selenium absorption','Transport iron and chromium','Limit excess entry of iron and zinc, respectively','Deliver iron and copper to liver'],a:2,e:'Ferritin sequesters iron in enterocytes, while metallothionein can bind zinc and limit transfer to blood.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:28,q:'Which inherited disorder reduces zinc absorption and causes infant dermatitis and growth failure?',o:['Acrodermatitis enteropathica','Hereditary hemochromatosis','Cretinism','Keshan disease'],a:0,e:'Acrodermatitis enteropathica is caused by impaired intestinal zinc transport.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:29,q:'Which nonessential mineral is recognized for strengthening teeth and supporting bone?',o:['Fluoride','Magnesium','Zinc','Molybdenum'],a:0,e:'Fluoride is not generally classified as essential but appropriate exposure reduces dental caries.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:30,q:'Which statement presents arsenic as having a possible biological role?',o:['It controls calcium metabolism','It may act as a cofactor in some experimental systems','Fruit and nuts are recommended arsenic sources','It is an approved food additive'],a:1,e:'B reflects the source wording, but arsenic is not an established essential human nutrient and inorganic arsenic is toxic and carcinogenic. This item is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null}
